@article{ WOS:001685337800001,
Author = {Ravi, Chandana and Sapra, Jayashree},
Title = {Boomerang hiring: a multi criterion decision making approach to identify
   critical factors to rehire former employees in automotive industry},
Journal = {INTERNATIONAL JOURNAL OF PRODUCTIVITY AND PERFORMANCE MANAGEMENT},
Year = {2026},
Volume = {75},
Number = {6},
Pages = {2212-2229},
Month = {JUN 16},
Abstract = {PurposeThe study aims to understand the concept of boomerang hiring: the
   rehiring of previous employees, within the automotive industry. It seeks
   to recognize and approve the key criteria that impact rehire choices and
   offer an organized, data-driven approach to assist HR experts in making
   educated choices. The consideration highlights the significance of
   coordinating human knowledge into AI-based decision models for a way
   better talent management.Design/methodology/approachThis study embraces
   a two-phase MCDM approach, using Fuzzy AHP to weigh ten key rehire
   criteria and Fuzzy TOPSIS to rank previous workers. The model's outputs
   are validated by comparing them with expert HR judgments. This
   integrated system improves objectivity in boomerang hiring choices and
   offers an organized method for ability re-evaluation. It aims to support
   key HR planning within the automotive industry. The study also conducted
   a consistency ratio (CR) test to verify the logical reliability of
   expert pairwise judgments within FAHP. Further, a +/- 10\% sensitivity
   analysis and bootstrap resampling (B = 2000) were applied to assess the
   robustness and stability of the FAHP-derived weights and FTOPSIS
   rankings. These validation steps ensure that the model results are
   consistent and statistically reliable under expert judgment
   uncertainty.FindingsThe study identifies attitude, domain knowledge and
   break period as the most compelling criteria in rehire choices. It also
   reveals that while intelligent models like FAHP-TOPSIS offer organized
   assessment, they cannot fully replace the subtlety of human judgment.
   Moreover, practices such as positive offboarding and maintaining alumni
   networks were found to upgrade the success rate of rehires. The results
   of the sensitivity and bootstrap analyses confirmed that these key
   criteria remained stable even when the weights were perturbed or
   resampled, validating the robustness of the FAHP-FTOPSIS framework. This
   research also contributes to the existing knowledge of performance
   management by showing that using combined MCDM methods in HR decisions
   can make judgments more correct, with lower risks of bias, and allow for
   strategic assessments of returning employees. The study introduces a
   clear way of measuring talent value by embedding FAHP-FTOPSIS into the
   HR processes, thus supporting better and more reliable data-driven
   methods for performance management in the car industry.Research
   limitations/implicationsThis study provides valuable insights, but its
   limited emphasis on the automobile sector may restrict its
   generalizability. Furthermore, the examination of HR specialists may not
   reflect different views. Future studies should include a wider range of
   industries and use different samples. Subjective evaluations by experts
   may also influence the findings. Future research might combine
   longitudinal data to increase reliability and depth. Although
   consistency, sensitivity and bootstrap validations were performed to
   enhance reliability, the model remains dependent on expert-based
   judgments, which introduces an element of subjectivity. Future studies
   can integrate larger datasets or multi-sectoral validations to further
   strengthen generalizability.Social implicationsBy pushing for a more
   inclusive approach to representative rehiring, this paper contributes to
   the broader discussion around workforce stability, engagement and the
   significance of positive organizational culture in retaining talented
   individuals within the industry.
   Originality/valueThis research fills a gap within the existing
   literature on boomerang hiring by offering an organized decision-making
   model utilizing FAHP and Fuzzy TOPSIS within the HR domain. It gives a
   novel, data-driven approach to rehire decision-making, merging
   analytical thoroughness with real-world HR insights. The study is
   particularly valuable for businesses like automotive where retaining
   skilled talent is critical. Furthermore, this study enhances
   methodological originality by incorporating statistical validation
   through CR, sensitivity and bootstrap analyses, establishing the
   FAHP-FTOPSIS model as a robust decision-support tool for HR
   applications.},
